Hello! We're finally here with a PR for #10040.

This PR adds an exported page function called `permuteStaticPaths`, similar to `getStaticProps` and `getStaticPaths`. It accepts a single arg — an array including one or more static paths — and returns a promise that will explode the path(s) combinatorially.

For example, consider a page you would like to statically generate in multiple languages:

```js
// pages/index.js
import IndexComponent from 'components/Index';

const LANGUAGES = ['en', 'es'];

export const Index = ({ language }) => (
  <IndexComponent language={language} />
);

export function getStaticProps({ params }) {
  const language = params.path.split('__')[1];

  return {
    props: { language },
  };
}

export function permuteStaticPaths(paths) {
  return paths.reduce(
    (languagePaths, path) => [
      ...languagePaths,
      ...LANGUAGES.map((language) => `${path}__${language}`),
    ],
    []
  );
}

export default Index;
```

We have built a companion lib to this PR focused on A/B testing called [next-experiments](https://github.com/Sheertex/next-experiments/tree/canary); there is [a sample project](https://github.com/Sheertex/next-experiments/tree/canary/samples/next-ab-testing) in the repo which we will happily contribute to the `examples` directory as well. The lib adds `<Experiment>` and `<Variant>` components, which work with Webpack & Babel plugins to:

1. Automatically identify any use of `<Experiment>` and `<Variant>` components on built pages
2. Create an `experiments.json` summary file that describes each page's possible experiment + variant combinations
3. Export a helper function to run in (or as!) `permuteStaticPaths`, which reads the `experiments.json` file per-page and returns the relevant experiment combinations (e.g. if a page has two experiments, one with variants `A|B` and the other with `A|B|C`, we would return results for `A[A|B|C]` and `B[A|B|C]`.

We are already using this library to power our new website build-out, and are nearly through integrating it with CI, routing requests at the CDN layer to the appropriate files at the origin. For example, using the languages scenario described above, you may want to route requests for `index.html` and `index.json` to their respective `index__en` or `index__es` variants based on the `Accept-Language` header to serve statically precompiled + internationalized payloads for your users.

Feedback is very much welcome && encouraged. We are excited to contribute back to a project that has contributed so much productivity to our own team!

